This is interesting because it really looks like it's a bug. If you do the same with `Subject` it works as expected and the observer is removed. I tried this demo with a couple of other variants: https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-9kvrfk?file=src/app/app.component.ts

However the reason why it doesn't work as expected is different than you describe. It's not tied to any specific subscriber like `FilterSubscriber` or `MapSubscriber` but generally any `Subscription` instance. 

For example all of these work as should:

```
const ee1 = new EventEmitter();
ee1.subscribe().unsubscribe();
console.log(ee1.observers.length); // 0

const ee2 = new EventEmitter();
ee2.asObservable().pipe(filter(() => true)).subscribe().unsubscribe();
console.log(ee2.observers.length); // 0

const ee3 = new EventEmitter();
ee3.subscribe(val => console.log(val)).unsubscribe();
console.log(ee3.observers.length); // 0
```

Note that I can use `asObservable()` and then chain operators and they'll unsubscribe correctly.

The problem is in the way `EventEmitter` works with instances of the `Subscription` class. This includes `FilterSubscriber`, `MapSubscriber`, etc.

Right now `EventEmitter.subscribe()` takes the `Subscription` object and wraps all its methods with functions `schedulerFn`, `errorFn` and `completeFn` and then passes them as parameters into `super.subscribe(...)`. This calls `Observable.subscribe()` where these [functions are wrapped into another `Subscriber` object](https://github.com/ReactiveX/rxjs/blob/master/src/internal/Observable.ts#L196) which is then returned. But it doesn't do anything with the original `Subscription` object that came to `EventEmitter.subscribe()` at the first place. It only returns the wrapped `Subscription` from  `Observable.subscribe()` so when it comes to disposing the Rx chain the original `Subscription` is never disposed.

For example these two examples won't work as should:

```
const ee = new EventEmitter();
ee.pipe(map(() => true)).subscribe().unsubscribe();
console.log('EventEmitter', ee.observers.length); // 1 - wrong

// This simulates `FilterSubscriber` or `MapSubscriber`
const subscriber = new Subscriber(val => {});
subscriber.add(() => console.log('Subscriber disposed')); // This is never called - wrong
const ee2 = new EventEmitter();
ee2.subscribe(subscriber).unsubscribe();
console.log('EventEmitter - Subscriber', ee2.observers.length); // 0
// This did unsubscribe because we're holding the direct `Subscription` for `EventEmitter`
// but didn't trigger the teardown function. The teardown function is
// where it unsubscribes from its parent in `FilterSubscriber` or `MapSubscriber`.
```

I think the easiest way to fix this and keep the original functionality without introducing any possible breaking changes is to add the "new" `Subscription` to the "original" one. hen when disposing the chain it'll properly dispose all `Subscription`s.

```
subscribe(generatorOrNext?: any, error?: any, complete?: any): any {
    // ...

    const sink = super.subscribe(schedulerFn, errorFn, completeFn);

    if (generatorOrNext instanceof Subscription) {
      generatorOrNext.add(sink);
    }

    return sink;
}
```

